BT 1 0 0 1 0 201.6000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (The second introductory lecture continues the review of soft-skills development, with attention to the) Tj T* (connection between self-awareness, personal habits and personality development. Self-awareness) Tj T* (means observing one's own behaviour, recognising strengths and limitations, and understanding) Tj T* (how one's actions affect other people. It provides a starting point for improvement because a person) Tj T* (cannot make a useful change without first noticing what needs to change. Personality development) Tj T* (is not limited to appearance or the way a person speaks. It also includes attitudes, habits,) Tj T* (responsibility, confidence and the ability to respond appropriately in different situations. Small) Tj T* (everyday actions can influence how others experience our behaviour. For example, listening without) Tj T* (interrupting, arriving prepared, completing promised tasks and accepting suggestions demonstrate) Tj T* (consideration and reliability. Reflection makes these habits easier to improve. Instead of judging an) Tj T* (entire day as successful or unsuccessful, a learner can examine one specific interaction. They can) Tj T* (ask what went well, what could have been handled differently and what they might try next time.) Tj T* (This turns experience into useful feedback. Self-review should be honest but constructive: the) Tj T* (purpose is to identify a practical next step, not to become discouraged by mistakes. The central idea) Tj T* (is that personal growth requires attention over time. A short reflection carried out regularly can) Tj T* (reveal patterns that may otherwise go unnoticed.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
