BT 1 0 0 1 0 201.6000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (A mindset is a collection of beliefs, attitudes and habitual ways of thinking that influences how a) Tj T* (person interprets situations and responds to them. Two people can face the same circumstances) Tj T* (but react differently because they view the situation in different ways. The shoe-company example) Tj T* (illustrates this point: two representatives visit an island where nobody wears shoes. One sees the) Tj T* (absence of customers as a reason that selling shoes is impossible, while the other sees a possible) Tj T* (new market. The situation is the same, but their interpretations differ. The lecture introduces) Tj T* (different ways of describing mindsets, including positive and negative, abundance and scarcity,) Tj T* (defence and offence, and fixed and growth mindsets. These categories describe different patterns of) Tj T* (thinking. A mindset can influence whether a person notices possibilities, avoids challenges,) Tj T* (welcomes feedback or repeats familiar behaviour without learning. A restrictive mindset may create) Tj T* (mental inertia, where a person continues acting in the same way even when the approach is not) Tj T* (producing good results. Understanding mindset is useful because thoughts can affect actions. If a) Tj T* (learner assumes that a difficult subject is beyond their ability, they may avoid practising it. If they) Tj T* (view difficulty as a signal to learn more, they are more likely to ask questions, try another method) Tj T* (and improve. This does not mean that every problem is easy or that positive thinking guarantees) Tj T* (success. It means that the way a problem is interpreted can influence the next action taken.) Tj T* ET
