BT 1 0 0 1 0 201.6000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (This lecture considers mindset specifically in the context of learning. Some attitudes can restrict) Tj T* (learning, while others encourage effort, adjustment and progress. A restrictive attitude may lead a) Tj T* (learner to believe that ability is fixed, that mistakes should be avoided or that asking questions is a) Tj T* (sign of weakness. Such beliefs can make a person less willing to attempt unfamiliar tasks. In) Tj T* (contrast, a learning-oriented attitude treats ability as something that can be developed through) Tj T* (practice, suitable strategies and feedback. A useful learning mindset does not mean that effort alone) Tj T* (is enough. Learners also need to examine whether their method is effective. If an approach does not) Tj T* (work, repeating it without reflection may not produce improvement. A better response is to identify) Tj T* (the difficulty, try a different explanation or strategy, and check the result. Feedback is valuable) Tj T* (because it points to a gap between the current performance and the intended outcome. This) Tj T* (approach is relevant to technical subjects. A programming error is not simply a sign of failure; it can) Tj T* (provide information about an incorrect assumption, an overlooked condition or a misunderstanding) Tj T* (of syntax. Reading the error message, testing a smaller example and reviewing the logic can help) Tj T* (reveal the cause. The same principle applies to communication: if an explanation is unclear to a) Tj T* (listener, the speaker can reorganise the points and try again. Learning therefore involves active) Tj T* (participation, patience and a willingness to revise one's approach.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
