BT 1 0 0 1 0 201.6000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (The lecture introduces time management by examining why time matters and how people perceive) Tj T* (and value it. Time is a limited resource: once a period has passed, it cannot be recovered. However,) Tj T* (people do not always use or experience time in the same way. An activity may feel short when a) Tj T* (person is fully engaged and much longer when attention is divided or the task is unclear. Personal) Tj T* (habits, priorities and distractions can therefore influence how effectively time is used. The first step) Tj T* (towards managing time is awareness. Before changing a routine, it is useful to observe how a) Tj T* (normal day is spent. A learner may discover that some periods are used productively, while other) Tj T* (periods are lost through avoidable interruptions, unclear planning or switching repeatedly between) Tj T* (activities. Recording activities for a short period can make these patterns easier to recognise. Time) Tj T* (management is not simply about filling every minute with work. Rest, meals, travel and recreation) Tj T* (are also part of a balanced routine. A realistic plan recognises that people need breaks and that) Tj T* (tasks may take longer than expected. The purpose of observing time use is to make informed) Tj T* (decisions about priorities, not to create an unnecessarily strict schedule. For a student, awareness) Tj T* (of time can help balance lectures, assignments, revision and personal activities. Once the actual) Tj T* (pattern is understood, it becomes easier to decide what needs more attention and where a small) Tj T* (adjustment could make the day more organised.) Tj T* ET
