BT 1 0 0 1 0 3.0000 Tm /F2 12 Tf 15 TL (Topic 5: Compare and Contrast Descriptive Essays) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 648.4200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 16.8000 Tm /F2 10.2 Tf 13.5 TL (Question 1: Electric Vehicles versus Conventional Internal Combustion Engine) Tj T* (Vehicles) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 620.0200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 16.8000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Electric vehicles \(EVs\) and internal combustion engine \(ICE\) vehicles are two different answers to) Tj T* (the same need, which is transport. Each has clear positives and negatives.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 539.6200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 69.6000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Consider battery life first. An EV battery usually lasts eight to ten years, but replacing it is very) Tj T* (expensive. In contrast, an ICE vehicle has no battery problem, though its engine needs regular) Tj T* (servicing. As for the manufacturing footprint, EVs create more emissions during production because) Tj T* (of battery mining and processing, whereas ICE vehicles have a lighter manufacturing footprint.) Tj T* (However, over its lifetime an EV produces far lower emissions, especially when the electricity comes) Tj T* (from renewable sources.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 481.6200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 43.2000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Operating costs also differ. EVs have fewer moving parts, so they need less maintenance, and) Tj T* (electricity per kilometre is cheaper than petrol or diesel. On the other hand, ICE vehicles cost less to) Tj T* (buy, and their repair shops are available everywhere. Fuel prices, however, keep rising and make) Tj T* (them costly to run.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 436.8200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 30.0000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Charging network feasibility is the biggest challenge for EVs. Charging stations are still limited,) Tj T* (especially on highways and in small towns, and charging takes much longer than refuelling. ICE) Tj T* (vehicles, conversely, can be refuelled in minutes at petrol stations spread across the country.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 396.0200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 30.0000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (In conclusion, EVs are cleaner and cheaper to run, whereas ICE vehicles are cheaper to buy and) Tj T* (more convenient at present. As battery prices fall and charging networks grow, EVs are likely to) Tj T* (become the better choice for the future.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 357.0200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 16.8000 Tm /F2 10.2 Tf 13.5 TL (Question 2: Remote Internships versus On-Site Internships for First-Year Engineering) Tj T* (Students) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 315.4200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 30.0000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Internships help early-stage engineering students connect classroom theory with real work. Today,) Tj T* (students can choose between remote \(virtual\) and on-site \(physical\) internships, and each has) Tj T* (advantages and disadvantages.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 261.4200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 43.2000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Hands-on skill acquisition is the biggest difference. On-site internships give direct access to) Tj T* (machines, laboratories and real equipment, so students learn practical skills such as wiring,) Tj T* (fabrication and testing. Remote internships, in contrast, mostly involve software, simulations and) Tj T* (online tasks, which are useful for coding and design but cannot replace physical practice.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 211.8200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 30.0000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (In terms of schedule flexibility, remote internships win clearly. Students can work from home, save) Tj T* (travel time and cost, and manage studies alongside the internship. On-site internships, on the other) Tj T* (hand, demand fixed hours and daily travel, which can be tiring during the semester.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 171.0200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 30.0000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Mentorship is stronger on-site, because supervisors can guide students directly and correct) Tj T* (mistakes immediately. Online mentors, however, are often busy and communicate only through) Tj T* (scheduled calls or messages, so doubts may stay unresolved for days.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 130.2200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 30.0000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Professional networking also favours on-site internships, as students meet colleagues, build) Tj T* (relationships and learn workplace culture naturally. Remote interns may never meet their team face) Tj T* (to face, although online platforms still allow them to connect with professionals from different cities.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 89.4200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 30.0000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (In conclusion, remote internships are better for flexibility and software skills, whereas on-site) Tj T* (internships are better for practical learning, mentorship and networking. A first-year student should) Tj T* (ideally begin with a remote internship and move to an on-site one later.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
endstream
