BT 1 0 0 1 0 16.8000 Tm /F2 10.2 Tf 13.5 TL (Question 3: Cloud Computing versus On-Premise Physical Data Centers for) Tj T* (Engineering Enterprises) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 709.6200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 16.8000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Engineering enterprises must decide whether to run their systems on the cloud or in their own) Tj T* (physical data centers. Both choices have strengths and weaknesses.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 655.6200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 43.2000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Upfront capital expenditure is the clearest difference. Cloud services need almost no initial) Tj T* (investment, because companies simply pay for what they use. On-premise data centers, in contrast,) Tj T* (require heavy spending on servers, cooling systems, power backup and buildings before any work) Tj T* (begins.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 597.6200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 43.2000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (As for recurring overheads, cloud bills can grow quickly if usage is not monitored, whereas an) Tj T* (on-premise setup has predictable costs once it is built, although it still needs staff, electricity and) Tj T* (maintenance. Therefore, cloud suits changing workloads, while on-premise suits stable and) Tj T* (long-term demand.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 539.6200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 43.2000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Workload scalability strongly favours the cloud. Companies can add computing power in minutes) Tj T* (during simulations or product launches and reduce it afterwards. On the other hand, on-premise) Tj T* (systems have fixed capacity, so expanding them takes weeks or months and leaves hardware idle) Tj T* (during quiet periods.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 481.6200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 43.2000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Data security and compliance are more complex. On-premise data centers give the company full) Tj T* (physical control, which is important for defence, healthcare or confidential design data. Cloud) Tj T* (providers offer strong security tools and certifications, but the company depends on a third party and) Tj T* (must follow data-location laws carefully.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 436.8200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 30.0000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (In conclusion, the cloud is better for flexibility, speed and low starting cost, while on-premise) Tj T* (infrastructure is better for control and predictable long-term expenses. Many enterprises now) Tj T* (choose a hybrid model that combines the advantages of both.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 397.8200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 16.8000 Tm /F2 10.2 Tf 13.5 TL (Question 4: 3D Printing \(Additive\) versus CNC Milling \(Subtractive\) in Prototype) Tj T* (Fabrication) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 356.2200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 30.0000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (In prototype fabrication, 3D printing and CNC milling follow opposite principles. A 3D printer builds a) Tj T* (part layer by layer, whereas a CNC machine cuts material away from a solid block. Each method) Tj T* (has positives and negatives.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 315.4200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 30.0000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Material wastage is the first point of contrast. 3D printing uses only the material needed for the part,) Tj T* (so wastage is low. CNC milling, on the other hand, removes a large amount of material as chips,) Tj T* (which increases the cost of expensive metals.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 274.6200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 30.0000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (When it comes to production speed, the picture changes with batch size. For single prototypes and) Tj T* (small batches, 3D printing is faster because it needs no tooling or setup. For larger batch runs,) Tj T* (however, CNC milling is quicker, as it can produce identical parts continuously at high speed.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 233.8200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 30.0000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Surface finish and accuracy favour CNC milling. It produces smooth surfaces and very tight) Tj T* (tolerances, so parts often need no extra work. In contrast, 3D printed parts often show layer lines) Tj T* (and need sanding or polishing, and their accuracy is lower.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 188.2200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 30.0000 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL (Geometric complexity is the strength of 3D printing. It can easily create hollow parts, internal) Tj T* (channels and lattice structures that are impossible to cut with a rotating tool. CNC milling is limited) Tj T* (by tool access and the direction of cutting.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 85.37008 147.4200 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 30 Tm /F1 9.6 Tf 13.2 TL -0.123812 Tw (In conclusion, 3D printing is ideal for complex, low-volume prototypes with little waste, whereas CNC) Tj T* 0 Tw (milling is better for accurate, smooth and larger-batch parts. Engineers often use both methods) Tj T* (together to get the best result.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
